WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
You've learned how to implement exponentially weighted averages. There's one technical detail called bias correction

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:11.160
that can make your computation of these averages more accurately. Let's see how that works.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.700
In the previous video you saw this figure for beta equals 0.9, this figure for beta equals

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.820
0.98, but it turns out that if you implement the formula as written here,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.960
you won't actually get the green curve when say beta equals 0.98.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.400
You actually get the purple curve here, and you notice that the purple curve starts off really low.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:42.440
So let's see how to fix that. When you're implementing a moving average, you initialize it with V0 equals 0,

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:50.280
and then V1 is equal to 0.98 V0 plus 0.02 theta 1.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.600
But V0 is equal to 0, so that term just goes away.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.240
So V1 is just 0.02 times theta 1.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.360
So that's why if the first day's temperature is

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:08.360
say 40 degrees Fahrenheit, then V1 will be 0.02 times 40, which is 0.8.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:13.720
So you get a much lower value down here. So it's not a very good estimate of the first day's temperature.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:21.160
V2 will be 0.98 times V1 plus 0.02 times theta 2.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:27.480
And if you plug in V1, which is this down here, and multiply it out,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:37.480
then you find that V2 is actually equal to 0.98 times 0.02 times theta 1 plus 0.02 times theta 2.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:45.720
And that's 0.0196 theta 1 plus 0.02 theta 2.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.720
So again, you know, assuming theta 1 and theta 2 are positive numbers,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:54.440
when you compute this, V2 will be much less than theta 1 or theta 2.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.640
So V2 isn't a very good estimate of the first two days temperature of the year.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:03.720
So it turns out that there's a way to modify this estimate that makes it much better,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.520
that makes it more accurate, especially during this initial phase of your estimate,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:16.360
which is that instead of taking Vt, take Vt divided by 1 minus beta to the power of t,

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.000
where t is the current day that you're on. So let's take a complete example.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:30.600
When t is equal to 2, 1 minus beta to the power of t is 1 minus 0.98 squared.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.920
And it turns out that this is 0.0396.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:46.760
And so your estimate of the temperature on day 2 becomes V2 divided by 0.0396.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:53.960
And this is going to be 0.0196 times theta 1 plus 0.02 theta 2.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:59.880
You notice that these two things add up to the denominator 0.0396.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.840
And so this becomes a weighted average of theta 1 and theta 2, and this removes this bias.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:13.160
So you notice that as t becomes large, beta to the t will become, will approach 0,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.240
which is why when t is large enough, the bias correction makes almost no difference.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.200
This is why when t is large, the purple line and the green line, you're pretty much overlapped.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.800
But during this initial phase of learning, when you're still warming up your estimates,

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.720
when bias correction can help you to obtain a better estimate of the temperature.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.440
And as this bias correction that helps you go from the purple line to the green line.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.480
So in machine learning, for most implementations of the exponentially weighted average,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:47.160
people don't often bother to implement bias correction, because most people would rather

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.320
just wait that initial period and have a slightly more bias estimate and go from there.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.600
But if you are concerned about the bias during this initial phase,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.800
while your exponentially weighted moving average is still warming up,

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.280
then bias correction can help you get a better estimate early on.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.240
So that you now know how to implement exponentially weighted moving averages.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:12.280
Let's go on and use this to build some better optimization algorithms.
